# Radio Tower — which Wi-Fi networks this station may join.
#
# Copy this file to the SD card's boot partition (the small one Windows can
# see, usually called "bootfs") and name it exactly:
#
#     radiotower-wifi.txt
#
# The tower reads it on EVERY boot. To move the station to a new house: put
# the card in a card reader, edit this file, put it back, power on. You never
# need to SSH in, and you never need a monitor and keyboard.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ONE NETWORK PER LINE:      network name | password
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Everything before the "|" is the network name, everything after it is the
# password. Spaces around the "|" are ignored, spaces inside the name and the
# password are kept. Lines starting with "#" are ignored, like this one.
#
# The FIRST line listed wins when more than one is in range, so put the
# network you actually want at the top.

Living Room 5G | correcthorsebattery

# A phone hotspot is a good second line — it means you can always reach the
# tower by turning tethering on, wherever you both happen to be.

# My iPhone | tetherpassword

# An open network with no password: leave the right-hand side empty.

# CoffeeShopGuest |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE FALLBACK: the tower makes its own Wi-Fi
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# If none of the networks above can be reached, the tower can broadcast its
# own instead. No router involved at all — people join the tower directly and
# open http://10.42.0.1:8080. This is what makes the station something you can
# carry to a place with no internet and still switch on.
#
# Uncomment the line below to enable it. The password must be at least 8
# characters, or WPA will refuse it.

# HOTSPOT = Radio Tower | towerpassword

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ONE HONEST WARNING
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The boot partition is FAT32 and has no permissions, so these passwords are
# readable by anyone who puts this card in a computer. That is the price of
# being able to fix the network without logging in. If the card is going
# somewhere you would not leave a written-down password, delete the lines
# after the first successful boot — NetworkManager has already stored them
# and the tower will keep reconnecting without this file.
